//! STEP STABILITY for the three-way loop — the `wstuck` half.
//!
//! ```text
//! wh3_step_stuck_stable : (loop monotonicity at budget k)
//!                       -> step at k on e = wstuck
//!                       -> step at k+1 on e = wstuck
//! ```
//!
//! ## What stability means here, and what it does not
//!
//! NOT that the step is the same at consecutive budgets. A `wstarved` step MAY
//! become `wstep` or `wstuck` with more fuel — that is what more fuel is for.
//! Only the INFORMATIVE verdicts are stable: `wstuck` stays `wstuck`, and
//! `wstep y` stays `wstep y`. `wstarved` is the bottom of an information order
//! and is allowed to move. Stating it as plain equality would be a false lemma
//! that looks right.
//!
//! ## Why it holds
//!
//! The step consults the pre-pass in exactly ONE place: level four of
//! `iota_reduct_whc3`, `wh major`. A `wstuck` verdict means the chain failed at
//! one of the other five levels. Four of those never mention the pre-pass; the
//! fifth reads `cname` off the pre-pass RESULT, so it depends on `wmajor` — and
//! `wmajor` is stable exactly by loop monotonicity at `k`, the hypothesis.
//!
//! For the two-way loop the same argument FAILS: a starved pre-pass returns
//! `none`, ι declines, and the verdict is indistinguishable from genuine
//! stuckness, so more fuel flips it. Here starvation has its own constructor and
//! cannot masquerade.
//!
//! ## The `app` arm was contested
//!
//! Two independent agents concluded this arm was not writable, reasoning that
//! `reduce_app_head_red_wh3` is a `KExpr.rec` on `f` and stays stuck while `f`
//! is a bound variable — so the whole head-shape family of the two-way
//! development would have to be rebuilt at `WhStepR` first. A third inlined it
//! with a NESTED `KExpr.rec` on `f`, giving each head shape its own convoy on
//! the head-reduct. That one elaborates. The disagreement was not resolvable by
//! reading; the build settled it.
//!
//! `DerivedProved`, empty axiom closure.

use crate::spec::error::SpecError;
use crate::spec::Specification;

impl Specification {
    /// The `wstuck` half of step stability.
    pub(super) fn add_wh3_step_stability(&mut self) -> Result<(), SpecError> {
        debug_assert!(
            Self::balanced(SRC_WH3_STEP_STUCK_STABLE),
            "wh3 stuck-stable parens"
        );
        self.add_recursive_def(
            SRC_WH3_STEP_STUCK_STABLE,
            "wh3_step_stuck_stable: a step that finds nothing to do at one budget still finds \
             nothing at the next. Nine arms, with the app arm carrying a nested nine-arm analysis \
             of the head and a convoy on the head-reduct inside each. \
             \
             The pre-pass enters at exactly one point, level four of the iota chain, so a wstuck \
             verdict rests on levels that either ignore the pre-pass or depend on it only through \
             wmajor — which the loop-monotonicity hypothesis pins. The corresponding statement for \
             the TWO-way loop is false, because there a starved pre-pass is indistinguishable from \
             genuine stuckness; separating them is what makes this provable. \
             \
             Half of what whnf_fuel_red_wh3_monotone needs. The other half, wstep stability, \
             CONSUMES this one at strict subterms. DerivedProved, zero axiom_deps.",
        )?;
        debug_assert!(
            Self::balanced(SRC_WH3_STEP_STEP_STABLE),
            "wh3 step-stable parens"
        );
        self.add_recursive_def(
            SRC_WH3_STEP_STEP_STABLE,
            "wh3_step_step_stable: a step that FIRES at one budget fires the same way at the \
             next — the second half of step stability, and the one that consumes the first. \
             \
             Nine-arm KExpr.rec on e with `forall y` INSIDE the motive, since the app and proj \
             arms instantiate the induction hypothesis at f2 / sub2. sort, bvar, lam, pi and lit \
             have premise `wstuck = wstep y`, which is impossible. const is opt_step_bind on \
             defval_for, budget-independent, so the premise IS the goal. let_ yields \
             `wstep (instantiate b v)` at both budgets, so the premise is the goal directly — no \
             wstep injectivity is needed, contrary to what the drafting brief anticipated. proj \
             convoys on the subterm step. \
             \
             The app arm carries a NESTED nine-arm analysis of the head, because \
             reduce_app_head_red_wh3 recurses on f and stays stuck while f is a bound variable — \
             the same technique the wstuck half established, after two independent agents \
             concluded the arm was unwritable and the build refuted them. Where the head-reduct is \
             wstuck it calls wh3_step_stuck_stable at that STRICT SUBTERM, which is why the two \
             halves are not siblings and why this one is registered second. \
             \
             With wh3_step_stuck_stable this completes step stability, whose two informative \
             verdicts are what whnf_fuel_red_wh3_monotone needs. DerivedProved, zero axiom_deps.",
        )?;
        self.add_wh3_loop_monotone()?;
        self.add_wh3_loop_le()?;
        Ok(())
    }

    /// The Le form of loop monotonicity.
    ///
    /// UNRESTRICTED, which is the whole difference. The two-way substitute
    /// `whnf_fuel_red_wh_le` carries a genuine-stuckness premise, because
    /// unrestricted monotonicity is false there — and that premise is free at
    /// `pi`/`lam` but NOT at `app`/`proj`, which is what made the completeness
    /// steps unportable. Here there is no premise to discharge.
    fn add_wh3_loop_le(&mut self) -> Result<(), SpecError> {
        debug_assert!(Self::balanced(SRC_WH3_LOOP_LE), "wh3 loop le parens");
        self.add_recursive_def(
            SRC_WH3_LOOP_LE,
            "whnf_fuel_red_wh3_le: a successful run survives raising the fuel to any Le-greater \
             bound, by Le.rec iterating whnf_fuel_red_wh3_monotone. \
             \
             No stuckness premise. whnf_fuel_red_wh_le needs one because unrestricted monotonicity \
             is FALSE for the two-way loop, and discharging it is free at pi and lam but not at \
             app or proj — which is precisely why defeq_complete_steps could not be ported. \
             DerivedProved, zero axiom_deps.",
        )?;
        Ok(())
    }

    /// MONOTONICITY for the three-way loop — the lemma the two-way loop cannot
    /// have.
    ///
    /// `whnf_fuel_red_wh_monotone_is_false` refutes exactly this statement for
    /// the two-way loop, by computation: a starved pre-pass makes ι decline, the
    /// loop announces a false stuck, and one more unit of fuel fires ι and then
    /// runs out — `some` at one budget, `none` at the next. Separating
    /// `wstarved` from `wstuck` removes that, because a `some` is now produced
    /// only where no rule applies at any budget.
    ///
    /// Plain `Nat.rec`, and the induction hypothesis IS the loop-monotonicity
    /// premise both stability halves take — so no `nat_strong_rec`, no
    /// `BoundedHSM`, no `LiftP` lift, unlike `wh_hsm_all`. The dependency is one
    /// step back, not strong induction.
    fn add_wh3_loop_monotone(&mut self) -> Result<(), SpecError> {
        debug_assert!(
            Self::balanced(SRC_WH3_LOOP_MONOTONE),
            "wh3 loop monotone parens"
        );
        self.add_recursive_def(
            SRC_WH3_LOOP_MONOTONE,
            "whnf_fuel_red_wh3_monotone: FUEL MONOTONICITY for the faithful loop, once starvation \
             and stuckness are distinguished. A successful run survives one more unit of fuel and \
             returns the same result. \
             \
             Nat.rec on the fuel. The zero arm is absurd, since the loop returns none there. The \
             successor arm convoys on the step: wstarved makes the premise absurd because dispatch \
             yields none; wstuck means dispatch returned the term itself, so the two endpoints \
             coincide and stuck-stability carries the verdict forward; wstep hands off to the loop \
             at the lower fuel, which the induction hypothesis lifts, with step-stability keeping \
             the reduct fixed. \
             \
             This is the exact statement whnf_fuel_red_wh_monotone_is_false denies for the TWO-way \
             loop, and the four layers of the completeness chain that were blocked — \
             defeq_fuel_mono, half of fuel_pairing, defeq_complete_steps, defeq_capstone — each \
             close a step with its analogue. Blocked by falsity before; available now. \
             DerivedProved, zero axiom_deps.",
        )?;
        Ok(())
    }
}

